{"reference":"2 Timothy 2","book":"2 Timothy","chapter":2,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter portrays teaching as something to be passed on: what Timothy heard is to be entrusted to reliable people who can teach others. Alongside this charge, the faithful saying speaks of dying and living, enduring and reigning, denial, and God’s continuing faithfulness. The large-house image then calls for cleansing, holiness, usefulness, and readiness for good work.","verse_refs":["2 Timothy 2:1-2","2 Timothy 2:20-21","2 Timothy 2:8-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_timothy_002_93b2f636eaed1b926857f0f5"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Images and disputed teaching","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The soldier, athlete, and farmer images emphasize focused endurance, integrity, labor, and hope of fruit. Later, the approved worker is contrasted with empty speech: orthotomeō describes the worker’s treatment of “the word of truth.” Hymenaeus and Philetus are identified as teaching that the resurrection had already occurred, a claim said to unsettle some people’s faith. Against this, the firm foundation joins God’s knowledge of his own with a call to turn from wickedness.","verse_refs":["2 Timothy 2:14-19","2 Timothy 2:15-16","2 Timothy 2:19","2 Timothy 2:3-7"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_timothy_002_5ba44671a15c1036e8050120"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Correcting without quarrelling","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The Lord’s servant is not simply told to reject foolish disputes. The passage sets out a manner of correction: avoid quarrelling, teach capably, endure wrong, and correct opponents gently, with hope that they may come to repentance and knowledge of the truth.","verse_refs":["2 Timothy 2:22-26"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_timothy_002_342c87607d6c3d4636d8ecef"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The letter’s broader shape","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Across the letter, its farewell setting, concern for succession, use of exemplary memory, warnings, final charge, and expectation of death make comparison with a testament plausible. That comparison remains disputed, both in its formal classification and in questions of historicity.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_timothy_002_d6dfdb83a087566129fcd81e"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}